 \begin{abstract}
-Does adding context hurt a small language model, and do the location and composition of that context matter? We test the frozen \modelName{} artifact on \sampleSize{} deterministic synthetic contract-clause probes spanning mean prompt lengths of \tierTwoTokens{}, \tierFourTokens{}, and \tierEightTokens{} tokens. Moving the same answer-bearing clause from the start to the end raised accuracy by \positionTwoStartEndChange{} percentage points at the shortest tier and \positionFourStartEndChange{} points at the middle tier, while keyword pruning changed matched accuracy by \pruningChange{} points. Removing only the lexical decoy from each pruned prompt then changed accuracy by \pruningNoDecoyChange{} points. The matched amount comparison was inconclusive: accuracy rose by \amountSharedTwoFourChange{} points and then changed by \amountSharedFourEightChange{} points, with only \amountSharedEightCount{} shared cases at each tier. Code-heavy business filler reduced accuracy relative to unrelated filler, but the manipulation does not isolate topical relevance from its other differences.
+Does adding context hurt a small language model, and do the location and composition of that context matter? We evaluate the frozen \modelName{} artifact on \sampleSize{} synthetic contract-clause probes. Deterministic runs span mean prompt lengths of \tierTwoTokens{}, \tierFourTokens{}, and \tierEightTokens{} tokens. Moving the same answer-bearing clause from the start to the end raised accuracy by \positionTwoStartEndChange{} percentage points at the shortest tier and \positionFourStartEndChange{} points at the middle tier, while keyword pruning changed matched accuracy by \pruningChange{} points. Removing only the lexical decoy from each pruned prompt then changed accuracy by \pruningNoDecoyChange{} points. The matched comparison across prompt lengths was inconclusive: accuracy rose by \amountSharedTwoFourChange{} points and then changed by \amountSharedFourEightChange{} points, with only \amountSharedEightCount{} shared cases at each tier. Code-heavy business filler reduced accuracy relative to unrelated filler, but the manipulation does not isolate topical relevance from its other differences.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \section{Introduction}
 
 You paste a long contract into a chatbot and ask, ``What code renews the vendor agreement?'' The answer sits in one clause, but a similar buyer clause repeats the words in your question and gives a different code.
 
-Long prompts make this ordinary lookup less predictable. A model may fail because the document is longer, because the answer appears in a weak position, or because nearby text resembles the question. Those explanations imply different choices: shorten the prompt, move the key clause, or filter the document. A useful evaluation must vary each property while holding the others fixed.
+This ordinary lookup becomes less predictable in a long prompt. The model may fail because it must process a longer document, because the answer appears in a weak position, or because nearby text resembles the question. Each explanation suggests a different response: shorten the prompt, move the key clause, or filter the document. Distinguishing among them requires an evaluation that varies a single property while holding the others fixed.
 
 We study these choices in a synthetic, gradeable version of the contract situation. Each document contains an answer-bearing vendor clause and a look-alike clause for another company. The question uses the wording of the look-alike clause, while the answer clause expresses the same relation with different words. This construction reduces the value of literal matching, following the motivation of NoLiMa \citep{modarressi2025nolima}.
 
-Our contribution is a fully paired, laptop-scale evaluation of a frozen open model. Prior work established position and length failures in larger systems \citep{liu2023lost,levy2024same}, and showed that irrelevant statements can disrupt reasoning \citep{shi2023distracted}. We compare code-heavy business filler with unrelated prose at matched prompt lengths, positions, and questions. We also test whether a deterministic keyword filter helps the exact cases on which the unfiltered model was evaluated.
+We provide a paired evaluation of a frozen open model at laptop scale. Prior work established position and length failures in larger systems \citep{liu2023lost,levy2024same} and showed that irrelevant statements can disrupt reasoning \citep{shi2023distracted}. Our comparisons match prompt lengths, answer positions, and questions while varying whether the filler is code-heavy business text or unrelated prose. We also apply a deterministic keyword filter to the same cases evaluated with the full prompt.
 
 The experiment asks four questions:
 \begin{enumerate}
-    \item As the document grows from \tierTwoTokens{} to \tierEightTokens{} mean tokens, how much does accuracy change on shared cases?
+    \item As mean prompt length grows from \tierTwoTokens{} to \tierEightTokens{} tokens, how much does accuracy change on shared cases?
     \item How does moving the same answer clause among the start, middle, and end change accuracy?
     \item How does accuracy differ between code-heavy business filler and unrelated prose in matched documents?
     \item Does keyword pruning recover accuracy, and can answer removal explain its failures?
 \end{enumerate}
 
-The answer is not a general context-length threshold. In this grid, the model improved when the answer appeared near the end, code-heavy business filler produced lower accuracy than unrelated prose, and keyword pruning amplified the lexical trap built into the probes.
+The experiment does not identify a general context-length threshold. Within this grid, accuracy was higher when the answer appeared near the end and lower with code-heavy business filler than with unrelated prose. Keyword pruning amplified the lexical trap built into the probes.
 
 \section{Related Work}
 
-\citet{liu2023lost} found a U-shaped position curve in multi-document question answering and key-value retrieval, with stronger use of information near context boundaries. Our design performs a smaller clause-reordering test: the question, filler sequence, clauses, and codes remain fixed while the answer clause is inserted at a different depth. Inserting it before the competing clause shifts that clause's final sentence index by one. The observed pattern is primarily recency rather than a full U-shape because the start condition is also weak.
+\citet{liu2023lost} found a U-shaped position curve in multi-document question answering and key-value retrieval, with stronger use of information near context boundaries. We conduct a smaller clause-reordering test in which the question, filler sequence, clauses, and codes remain fixed while we insert the answer clause at a different depth. When we place it before the competing clause, the competing clause's final sentence index shifts by one. The resulting pattern mainly favors recent information rather than forming a full U-shape, because accuracy is also low in the start condition.
 
-Input amount can matter even when the underlying problem is fixed. \citet{levy2024same} padded matched reasoning problems and observed degradation well before model context limits, while RULER found that usable context lengths can be shorter than advertised windows \citep{hsieh2024ruler}. Our amount comparison uses the intersection of cases present at every tier, avoiding a change in scenario mix as prompts grow.
+Prompt length can matter even when the underlying problem is fixed. \citet{levy2024same} padded matched reasoning problems and observed degradation well before model context limits, while RULER found that usable context lengths can be shorter than advertised windows \citep{hsieh2024ruler}. Our prompt-length comparison includes only cases present at every tier, so the mix of scenarios remains fixed as prompts grow.
 
-\citet{shi2023distracted} added irrelevant statements to arithmetic problems and found lower reasoning accuracy. We compare filler from the same business domain with unrelated prose while matching amount and question wording. The business condition also contains more entities and code-shaped candidates, so the comparison measures that bundle rather than topical relevance alone. NoLiMa shows why literal needle tests can overstate retrieval ability \citep{modarressi2025nolima}; our answer clause therefore paraphrases the question while a competing clause shares its surface form. The tested model belongs to the instruction-tuned Qwen family \citep{qwen2024qwen25}.
+\citet{shi2023distracted} added irrelevant statements to arithmetic problems and found lower reasoning accuracy. We compare filler from the same business domain with unrelated prose while matching prompt length and question wording. The business condition also contains more entities and code-shaped candidates, so the comparison measures that bundle rather than topical relevance alone. NoLiMa shows that synthetic retrieval tests based on literal word matches can overstate retrieval ability \citep{modarressi2025nolima}. We therefore paraphrase the question in the answer clause while giving the competing clause the same surface wording as the question. The tested model belongs to the instruction-tuned Qwen family \citep{qwen2024qwen25}.
 
